t them to \\(0\\)) leaves \\(k\\) non‑negative numbers \\(b_i=x_{2i}\\) with \\(\\sum b_i^{2}\\le2k\\).  \n   By the QM–AM inequality,\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=1}^{k}b_i\\Bigr)^{2}\\le k\\sum_{i=1}^{k}b_i^{2}\\le k\\cdot2k,\n   \\]\n   whence \\(\\sum b_i\\le k\\sqrt2\\). Equality is achieved by taking every even move equal to \\(\\sqrt2\\) and every odd move equal to \\(0\\).\n\n5. **Threshold for Alice.**  \n   Using (1) in condition (A) we need  \n   \\[\n   (4k-3)\\lambda^{2}>2k\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   \\lambda^{2}>\\frac{2k}{4k-3}.\n   \\]\n   The right–hand side decreases to \\(\\tfrac12\\) as \\(k\\to\\infty\\).  \n   Hence Alice can force a win exactly when \\(\\lambda^{2}>\\tfrac12\\), i.e. \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n6. **Threshold for Bazza.**  \n   Using (2) in condition (B) we need  \n   \\[\n   k\\sqrt2>\\lambda(2k+1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   \\sqrt2>\\lambda\\Bigl(2+\\frac1k\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   Letting \\(k\\to\\infty\\) shows that this is possible precisely when \\(\\sqrt2>2\\lambda\\), i.e. \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n7. **Borderline case \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).**  \n   For this value the inequalities in (A) and (B) are never strict, so neither player can force a win.  \n   An explicit infinite play is given by  \n   \\[\n   x_{2k-1}=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\;(k\\ge1),\\qquad x_{2k}=0\\;(k\\ge1).\n   \\]\n   Then for all \\(k\\)\n   \\[\n   S_{2k-1}=k\\lambda\\le\\lambda(2k-1),\\qquad\n   Q_{2k}=k\\lambda^{2}=k/2\\le2k,\n   \\]\n   so the rules are never violated and the game proceeds forever.\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i,\\qquad Q_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe rules of the game are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd: }&S_n\\le\\lambda n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even: }&Q_n\\le n .\n\\end{cases}\\tag{R}\n\\]\n\nA player loses on his turn exactly when the opponent’s inequality is already violated even if he would answer \\(x_n=0\\); therefore\n\n\