. Suppose we set odd moves to their maximum allowed by odd-turn constraint: at odd turn 1, we can set x_1 ≤ lambda * 1 = 0.5. So a_1 ≤ 0.5. At odd turn 3, we have S_3 ≤ lambda * 3 = 1.5. So a_2 + b_1 + a_1 ≤ 1.5. If we set b_1 = 0 (to maximize even moves later?), then a_2 ≤ 1.5 - a_1 = 1.0. So a_2 ≤ 1.0. At odd turn 5, S_5 ≤ lambda * 5 = 2.5. So a_3 + b_2 + a_2 + b_1 + a_1 ≤ 2.5. If we set b_1 = b_2 = 0, then a_3 ≤ 2.5 - a_1 - a_2 = 2.5 - 0.5 - 1.0 = 1.0. So a_3 ≤ 1.0. So odd moves can be up to 0.5, 1.0, 1.0, etc. So sum a_j grows roughly linearly with k, maybe ~ (k-1) * lambda*2? Actually from Lemma 1, for lambda > 0, a_j = 2 lambda for j>=2, a_1 = lambda. So sum a_j ≈ (2 lambda) (k-1) + lambda = (2 lambda k - lambda). For lambda = 0.5, sum a_j ≈ (2*0.5*k - 0.5) = (k - 0.5). So sum a_j ≈ k - 0.5. So sum a_j is about k. Meanwhile, sum b_j ≤ sqrt(k (2k - sum a_j^2)). If sum a_j ≈ k, then sum a_j^2 ≈ k^2. Then sum b_j ≤ sqrt(k (2k - k^2)) = sqrt(2k^2 - k^3). For large k, this becomes imaginary (since 2k - k^2 negative). Actually sum a_j^2 cannot exceed 2k because sum of squares ≤ 2k. So sum a_j^2 ≤ 2k. If sum a_j ≈ k, then sum a_j^2 is at least (sum a_j)^2 / k = k^2 / k = k. So sum a_j^2 ≈ k (maybe larger). So sum b_j ≤ sqrt(k (2k - sum a_j^2)). If sum a_j^2 is close to 2k, sum b_j is small. So total sum S_{2k} = sum a_j + sum b_j ≤ sum a_j + sqrt(k (2k - sum a_j^2)). This is less than or equal to sum a_j + sqrt(k (2k - sum a_j^2)). For sum a_j = k, sum a_j^2 = at least k (maybe more). Then sqrt(k (2k - sum a_j^2)) ≤ sqrt(k (2k - k)) = sqrt(k^2) = k. So S_{2k} ≤ k + k = 2k. So S_{2k} ≤ 2k, which is larger than k sqrt(2). So the bound k sqrt(2) is not necessarily true.\n\nBut we need to see if for lambda 1/sqrt(2), the condition S_{2k} > lambda (2k+1) can be satisfied. The solution uses bound S_{2k} ≤ k sqrt(2) to derive condition sqrt(2) > 2 lambda. If S_{2k} could be larger than k sqrt(2), then the condition might be weaker, maybe allowing Bazza to win for larger lam