e  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i\\le\\lambda n, \\tag{A}\n\\]\n\nand for even \\(n\\) Bazza must ensure  \n\n\\[\nQ_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}\\le n. \\tag{B}\n\\]\n\nThe game ends when a player cannot choose a non‑negative real number satisfying the appropriate inequality; the other player then wins.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Alice’s winning region \\(\\boldsymbol{\\lambda>1}\\)\n\nAssume \\(\\lambda>1\\).  \nAlice adopts the following strategy:\n\n> On each odd turn \\(n=2k-1\\) she chooses \\(x_{2k-1}=\\lambda\\).\n\nBecause \\(x_{2k-1}\\ge0\\) and \\(\\lambda>0\\), this choice is admissible provided (A) holds at turn \\(2k-1\\).  \nWe verify by induction that (A) indeed holds:\n\n- For \\(k=1\\) (turn \\(n=1\\)) we have \\(S_1=\\lambda\\le\\lambda\\cdot1\\), so (A) is satisfied.\n- Suppose (A) holds after turn \\(2k-3\\). Then after Alice’s choice at turn \\(2k-1\\),\n\n\\[\nS_{2k-1}=S_{2k-3}+ \\lambda \\le \\lambda(2k-3)+\\lambda =\\lambda(2k-2)\\le\\lambda(2k-1),\n\\]\n\nso (A) continues to hold.\n\nThus Alice can always make the prescribed move.\n\nNow consider Bazza’s turn \\(n=2k\\).  \nLet \\(y_k:=x_{2k}\\) be Bazza’s choice.  \nFrom (B) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}y_i^{2}=Q_{2k}-\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\lambda^{2}\n=Q_{2k}-k\\lambda^{2}\\le 2k-k\\lambda^{2}=k(2-\\lambda^{2}). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince each \\(y_i\\ge0\\), the left–hand side of (1) is non‑negative, hence we must have \\(2-\\lambda^{2}\\ge0\\); otherwise (1) would be violated for sufficiently large \\(k\\).  \nIf \\(\\lambda>1\\) then \\(\\lambda^{2}>1\\) and consequently \\(2-\\lambda^{2}<1\\).  \n\nFrom (1) we obtain an upper bound for the total sum of Bazza’s numbers:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}y_i\\le\\sqrt{k\\sum_{i=1}^{k}y_i^{2}}\n\\le\\sqrt{k\\cdot k(2-\\lambda^{2})}=k\\sqrt{2-\\lambda^{2}}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence after turn \\(2k\\),\n\n\\[\nS_{2k}=k\\lambda+\\sum_{i=1}^{k}y_i\\ge k\\lambda.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\lambda>1\\) then \\(k\\lamb