{2k-2}+x_{2k-1}^{2}>2k,\n   \\]\n   so Bazza cannot move at turn \\(2k\\). Alice wins.\n\n5. **Bazza\u2019s winning strategy for \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\).**  \n   *Lemma.* For any \\(p\\in[0,\\sqrt2]\\) define\n   \\[\n   f(p)=p+\\sqrt{\\,2-p^{2}\\,}.\n   \\]\n   One checks that \\(f(p)\\ge\\sqrt2\\) with equality only at \\(p=0\\) or \\(p=\\sqrt2\\).  \n\n   Bazza\u2019s strategy: after Alice\u2019s odd move \\(x_{2k-1}=p\\) (with \\(p^{2}\\le2\\)), he chooses\n   \\[\n   x_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2-p^{2}\\,}.\n   \\]\n   Then \\(Q_{2k}=2k\\) (the square\u2011budget is completely used) and the total sum increases by\n   \\[\n   S_{2k}=S_{2k-2}+p+x_{2k}=S_{2k-2}+f(p)\\ge S_{2k-2}+\\sqrt2 .\n   \\]\n   By induction,\n   \\[\n   S_{2k}\\ge k\\sqrt2 .\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\), there exists \\(k\\) with\n   \\[\n   k\\sqrt2>\\lambda(2k+1),\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(S_{2k}>\\lambda(2k+1)\\). At the next odd turn Alice cannot satisfy her sum\u2011bound, so Bazza wins.\n\n6. **The critical case \\(\\lambda=1/\\sqrt2\\).**  \n   Let Alice always choose \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\) and let Bazza always choose \\(x_{2k}=\\sqrt2\\).  \n   Then for every \\(k\\)\n   \\[\n   S_{2k}=k\\sqrt2=\\lambda(2k),\\qquad Q_{2k}=2k,\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   S_{2k}<\\lambda(2k+1).\n   \\]\n   Hence all constraints are satisfied forever; the game never ends.  \n   Because each player has a strategy that avoids losing, neither possesses a forced win.\n\nCombining the three parts yields the complete classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Alice wins  } \\iff\\ \\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{Bazza wins } \\iff\\ \\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{For }\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\text{ the game can be forced to continue forever.}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\;}\n\\]"
