da=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).**  \n   Let Alice always choose \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\) and Bazza always choose \\(x_{2k}=\\sqrt2\\).  Then for every \\(k\\)\n   \\[\n   Q_{2k}=2k,\\qquad S_{2k}=k\\sqrt2=\\lambda\\cdot2k,\n   \\]  \n   and\n   \\[\n   S_{2k-1}=(k-1)\\sqrt2\\le\\lambda(2k-1).\n   \\]  \n   All constraints are satisfied for all turns, so the game can continue indefinitely.  Because each player possesses a strategy that avoids losing, neither player has a forced winning strategy at \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\nCombining the three parts yields the complete classification stated in the boxed answer.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n**1. Preliminaries.**  \nFor each turn \\(n\\) define  \n\\[\nS_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i,\\qquad Q_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}.\n\\]  \nThe rules can be rewritten as:\n\n- At an odd turn \\(n=2k+1\\) Alice must pick \\(x_n\\ge0\\) with  \n  \\[\n  S_{2k}+x_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1).\n  \\]  \n  She fails (and loses) iff \\(S_{2k}>\\lambda(2k+1)\\).\n\n- At an even turn \\(n=2k\\) Bazza must pick \\(x_n\\ge0\\) with  \n  \\[\n  Q_{2k-1}+x_{2k}^{2}\\le2k.\n  \\]  \n  He fails (and loses) iff \\(Q_{2k-1}>2k\\).\n\nThus the game ends precisely when one of the two inequalities above is violated.\n\nA basic inequality that will be used repeatedly is the Cauchy\u2013Schwarz estimate  \n\\[\nS_n^{2}\\le n\\,Q_n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n**2. Alice\u2019s winning strategy when \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).**  \n\n*Lemma 1.*  Suppose Alice has played the value \\(0\\) on each of her previous turns.  After \\(k-1\\) complete pairs the sum of Bazza\u2019s numbers satisfies  \n\\[\nS_{2k-2}\\le\\sqrt2\\,(k-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]  \n\n*Proof of Lemma 1.*  The numbers \\(x_2,x_4,\\dots ,x_{2k-2}\\) are non\u2011negative, there are exactly \\(k-1\\) of them, and by Bazza\u2019s even\u2011turn constraint  \n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}x_{2i}^{2}\\le2(k-1).\n\\]  \nApplying the QM\u2013AM inequality (or (1) with \\(n=k-1\\)) gives  \n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}x_{2i}\\Bigr)^{2}\\le(k-1)\\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}x_{2i}^{2}\\le(k-1)\\cdot2(k-1),\n\\]  \nhence (2). \u220e\n\nBecause \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\), the sequence  \n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt2\\,k}{2k-1}\\qquad(k=1,2,\\dots)\n\\]  \ndecreases to \\(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).  Consequently there exists an integer \\(k\\) such that  \n\\[\n\\lambda(2k-1)-\\sqrt2\\,(k-1)>\\sqrt2. \\tag{3}\n\\]  \n\nAlice now follows the strategy:\n\n- On every odd turn before \\(2k-1\\) she chooses \\(x_n=0\\).\n- On turn \\(n=2k-1\\) she chooses the maximal admissible value  \n  \\[\n  x_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)-S_{2k-2}.\n  \\]  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f1, \\(S_{2k-2}\\le\\sqrt2\\,(k-1)\\); inequality (3) guarantees that the chosen number satisfies \\(x_{2k-1}>\\sqrt2\\), hence it is non\u2011negative.\n\nSince Bazza\u2019s constraint after the previous even turn gives \\(Q_{2k-2}\\le2(k-1)\\), we obtain  \n\\[\nQ_{2k-1}=Q_{2k-2}+x_{2k-1}^{2}>2(k-1)+2=2k.\n\\]  \nThus at the subsequent even turn \\(2k\\) Bazza cannot find a non\u2011negative \\(x_{2k}\\) with \\(Q_{2k-1}+x_{2k}^{2}\\le2k\\); he loses and Alice wins.\n\n**3. Bazza\u2019s winning strategy when \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).**  \n\n*Strategy.*  After Alice\u2019s odd move \\(x_{2k-1}=p\\) (with \\(p\\ge0\\)), Bazza selects  \n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2-p^{2}\\,}. \\tag{4}\n\\]  \n\nWe verify that this is always legal and forces a win.\n\n*Lemma 2.*  Throughout the game Alice\u2019s odd move always satisfies \\(p\\le\\sqrt2\\).  \n\n*Proof.*  The claim is proved by induction on the number of completed pairs.\n\n- **Base case (\\(k=1\\)).**  Before the first move we have \\(S_0=0\\); Alice\u2019s admissible choice satisfies \\(p\\le\\lambda<\\sqrt2\\).\n\n- **Inductive step.**  Assume after \\(k-1\\) pairs we have \\(Q_{2k-2}=2(k-1)\\) (true because of (4)) and, by Lemma\u202f4 below, \\(S_{2k-2}\\ge(k-1)\\sqrt2\\).  Alice\u2019s move must obey  \n  \\[\n  p\\le\\lambda(2k-1)-S_{2k-2}\\le\\lambda(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2.\n  \\]  \n  Since \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\), the right\u2011hand side does not exceed \\(\\sqrt2\\).  Hence \\(p\\le\\sqrt2\\). \u220e\n\n*Lemma 3.*  With the response (4) we have  \n\\[\nQ_{2k}=Q_{2k-2}+p^{2}+(2-p^{2})=Q_{2k-2}+2=2k. \\tag{5}\n\\]  \n\nThus Bazza exhausts exactly two units of square budget in each pair.\n\n*Lemma 4.*  Define \\(f(p)=p+\\sqrt{2-p^{2}}\\) for \\(p\\in[0,\\sqrt2]\\).  The function satisfies \\(f(p)\\ge\\sqrt2\\) (minimum at the endpoints).  Consequently, after \\(k\\) pairs the total sum obeys  \n\\[\nS_{2k}= \\sum_{i=1}^{k}f(p_i)\\ge k\\sqrt2. \\tag{6}\n\\]  \n\n*Winning condition.*  Because \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\), we have \\(2\\lambda<\\sqrt2\\).  Hence for sufficiently large \\(k\\),\n\\[\nk\\sqrt2 > \\lambda(2k+1).\n\\]  \nBy (6) this means \\(S_{2k}>\\lambda(2k+1)\\).  At the next odd turn \\(2k+1\\) Alice cannot satisfy the sum bound, so she loses and Bazza wins.\n\n**4. The critical value \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).**  \n\nConsider the following pair of strategies:\n\n- Alice always chooses \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\).\n- Bazza always chooses \\(x_{2k}=\\sqrt2\\).\n\nThen for every \\(k\\)\n\\[\nQ_{2k}=2k,\\qquad S_{2k}=k\\sqrt2=\\lambda\\cdot2k,\n\\]  \nand\n\\[\nS_{2k-1}=(k-1)\\sqrt2\\le\\lambda(2k-1).\n\\]  \nAll constraints are satisfied for all turns, so the game can be continued indefinitely.  Because each player has a strategy that avoids losing, neither player possesses a forced winning strategy when \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n**5. Conclusion.**  \nThe analysis above shows that the game\u2019s outcome is completely determined by the comparison of \\(\\lambda\\) with \\(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\), yielding the classification displayed in the boxed answer.\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, carefully review the **Summary** and **Detailed Solution** sections to ensure that every logical step is fully justified, all mathematical statements are expressed in proper TeX notation, and the answer adheres exactly to the required format and completeness. Verify that no gaps or unproven claims remain."
