ng his constraint\n\\[\nQ_{k-1}+a_k^{2}+b_k^{2}\\le 2k\n\\]\nis obtained by setting  \n\n\\[\nb_k=\\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,}\\ge 0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus, provided Alice respects \\(a_k\\le\\sqrt2\\), Bazza can always respond and the game proceeds to the next round.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma 1 – Square budget after a full round\n\n*Lemma.* If the game reaches the end of round \\(k\\) (i.e. after Bazza’s move), then  \n\n\\[\nQ_{k}=2k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* By induction.  For \\(k=0\\) the statement is true.  Assume it holds for \\(k-1\\).  \nBefore Alice’s move in round \\(k\\) we have \\(Q_{k-1}=2(k-1)\\).  \nShe adds \\(a_k^{2}\\); after that the sum of squares is \\(2(k-1)+a_k^{2}\\).  \nBazza then chooses \\(b_k\\) as in (1), giving  \n\n\\[\nQ_{k}=2(k-1)+a_k^{2}+b_k^{2}=2(k-1)+a_k^{2}+ \\bigl(2-a_k^{2}\\bigr)=2k .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\nConsequently, as long as the game continues, the equality \\(Q_{k}=2k\\) holds for every \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Increase of the total sum in a round\n\nFrom (1) the increase of the total sum in round \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_k:=a_k+b_k = a_k+\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}=:f(a_k),\\qquad 0\\le a_k\\le\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe function \\(f\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nf'(a)=1-\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}},\\qquad \nf'(a)=0\\iff a=1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(f\\) attains its maximum \\(f(1)=2\\) at \\(a=1\\) and its minimum\n\\[\nf_{\\min}=f(0)=f(\\sqrt2)=\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore for every round  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ \\sqrt2\\le\\Delta_k\\le2\\ } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Alice’s budget before her move\n\nBefore Alice’s move in round \\(k\\) the total sum already equals \\(S_{k-1}\\).  \nHer move must keep the overall sum within her own bound, i.e.\n\n\\[\nS_{k-1}+a_k\\le\\lambda(2k-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDefine the *available budget* for Alice on round \\(k\\) by  \n\n\\[\nB_k:=\\lambda(2k-1)-S_{k-1}\\ge 0 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\) she can choose a number \\(a_k>B_k-\\sqrt2>\\sqrt2\\) and, by (5), the move is legal; then by Lemma 1 the subsequent Bazza‑move is impossible because it would require a negative square‑budget.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Alice wins on round }k\\iff B_k>\\sqrt2.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for the total sum\n\nFrom (4) we obtain, by induction,  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}\\ge k\\sqrt2\\qquad(k\\ge0).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIndeed \\(S_0=0\\); assuming \\(S_{k-1}\\ge (k-1)\\sqrt2\\) and using (4),\n\n\\[\nS_{k}=S_{k-1}+\\Delta_k\\ge (k-1)\\sqrt2+\\sqrt2=k\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Determination of the critical value of \\(\\lambda\\)\n\nCombine (6) with (8):\n\n\\[\nB_k\\le \\lambda(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*If* \\(\\lambda>\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then the right‑hand side of (9) grows linearly with slope \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2>0\\).  Consequently there exists a smallest integer \\(k\\) for which \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\); by (7) Alice wins on that round.  \n\n*If* \\(\\lambda<\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2<0\\) and the right‑hand side of (9) eventually becomes negative.  Hence for some \\(k\\) we have \\(B_k<0\\); Alice then has no legal move and loses, so Bazza wins.  \n\n*If* \\(\\lambda=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2=0\\) and (9) yields the constant bound  \n\n\\[\n0<B_k\\le\\frac1{\\sqrt2}<\\sqrt2\\qquad\\text{for all }k .\n\\]\n\nThus Alice never obtains a winning budget, but she always has a legal move (e.g. \\(a_k=0\\)).  By always playing \\(a_k=0\\) she forces \\(b_k=\\sqrt2\\) each round, and the equalities (8) and (9) become equalities; the game can continue indefinitely.  Hence neither player possesses a winning strategy.\n\n------------------------------------------------------