ambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)*, Bazza always plays \\(b_k=\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}\\).  Since each round adds at least \\(\\sqrt2\\) to the sum, after \\(k-1\\) rounds \\(S_{k-1}\\ge (k-1)\\sqrt2\\).  Consequently \\(B_k\\le \\lambda(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2\\) becomes negative for large \\(k\\); Alice then has no legal move and loses, so Bazza wins.  \n\n*If \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)*, the inequality is never strict: \\(B_k\\equiv\\frac1{\\sqrt2}>0\\) while \\(B_k\\le\\sqrt2\\) for all \\(k\\).  By always taking \\(a_k=0\\) (hence \\(b_k=\\sqrt2\\)) the game proceeds forever, satisfying all constraints.  Neither player has a winning strategy.\n\nThus the threshold is \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda_c=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n**Final answer**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\lambda>\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\;:\\;\\text{Alice has a winning strategy},\\\\[4pt]\n\\lambda<\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\;:\\;\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy},\\\\[4pt]\n\\lambda=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\;:\\;\\text{neither player can force a win (the game can last forever).}\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]  \n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet the game be played in rounds indexed by \\(k=1,2,\\dots\\).  \nIn round \\(k\\)\n\n* Alice (odd turn \\(n=2k-1\\)) chooses a non\u2011negative real number \\(a_k\\).\n* Bazza (even turn \\(n=2k\\)) chooses a non\u2011negative real number \\(b_k\\).\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}= \\sum_{i=1}^{k}(a_i+b_i) \\qquad\\text{(total sum after round }k\\text{)},\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_{k}= \\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\bigl(a_i^{2}+b_i^{2}\\bigr) \\qquad\\text{(total sum of squares after round }k\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Behaviour of Bazza\u2019s move\n\nAssume the game has not ended before Bazza\u2019s move in round \\(k\\).  \nJust before his move the sum of squares equals  \n\n\\[\nQ_{k-1}=2(k-1) ,\n\\]\nbecause after each completed round the square\u2011budget is exactly exhausted (see Lemma\u202f1 below).  \n\nIf Alice chooses \\(a_k\\) with \\(0\\le a_k\\le\\sqrt2\\), the maximal value Bazza can take without violating his constraint\n\\[\nQ_{k-1}+a_k^{2}+b_k^{2}\\le 2k\n\\]\nis obtained by setting  \n\n\\[\nb_k=\\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,}\\ge 0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus, provided Alice respects \\(a_k\\le\\sqrt2\\), Bazza can always respond and the game proceeds to the next round.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma 1 \u2013 Square budget after a full round\n\n*Lemma.* If the game reaches the end of round \\(k\\) (i.e. after Bazza\u2019s move), then  \n\n\\[\nQ_{k}=2k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* By induction.  For \\(k=0\\) the statement is true.  Assume it holds for \\(k-1\\).  \nBefore Alice\u2019s move in round \\(k\\) we have \\(Q_{k-1}=2(k-1)\\).  \nShe adds \\(a_k^{2}\\); after that the sum of squares is \\(2(k-1)+a_k^{2}\\).  \nBazza then chooses \\(b_k\\) as in (1), giving  \n\n\\[\nQ_{k}=2(k-1)+a_k^{2}+b_k^{2}=2(k-1)+a_k^{2}+ \\bigl(2-a_k^{2}\\bigr)=2k .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\nConsequently, as long as the game continues, the equality \\(Q_{k}=2k\\) holds for every \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Increase of the total sum in a round\n\nFrom (1) the increase of the total sum in round \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_k:=a_k+b_k = a_k+\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}=:f(a_k),\\qquad 0\\le a_k\\le\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe function \\(f\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nf'(a)=1-\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}},\\qquad \nf'(a)=0\\iff a=1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(f\\) attains its maximum \\(f(1)=2\\) at \\(a=1\\) and its minimum\n\\[\nf_{\\min}=f(0)=f(\\sqrt2)=\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore for every round  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ \\sqrt2\\le\\Delta_k\\le2\\ } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Alice\u2019s budget before her move\n\nBefore Alice\u2019s move in round \\(k\\) the total sum already equals \\(S_{k-1}\\).  \nHer move must keep the overall sum within her own bound, i.e.\n\n\\[\nS_{k-1}+a_k\\le\\lambda(2k-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDefine the *available budget* for Alice on round \\(k\\) by  \n\n\\[\nB_k:=\\lambda(2k-1)-S_{k-1}\\ge 0 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\) she can choose a number \\(a_k>B_k-\\sqrt2>\\sqrt2\\) and, by (5), the move is legal; then by Lemma\u202f1 the subsequent Bazza\u2011move is impossible because it would require a negative square\u2011budget.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Alice wins on round }k\\iff B_k>\\sqrt2.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for the total sum\n\nFrom (4) we obtain, by induction,  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}\\ge k\\sqrt2\\qquad(k\\ge0).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIndeed \\(S_0=0\\); assuming \\(S_{k-1}\\ge (k-1)\\sqrt2\\) and using (4),\n\n\\[\nS_{k}=S_{k-1}+\\Delta_k\\ge (k-1)\\sqrt2+\\sqrt2=k\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Determination of the critical value of \\(\\lambda\\)\n\nCombine (6) with (8):\n\n\\[\nB_k\\le \\lambda(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*If* \\(\\lambda>\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then the right\u2011hand side of (9) grows linearly with slope \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2>0\\).  Consequently there exists a smallest integer \\(k\\) for which \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\); by (7) Alice wins on that round.  \n\n*If* \\(\\lambda<\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2<0\\) and the right\u2011hand side of (9) eventually becomes negative.  Hence for some \\(k\\) we have \\(B_k<0\\); Alice then has no legal move and loses, so Bazza wins.  \n\n*If* \\(\\lambda=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2=0\\) and (9) yields the constant bound  \n\n\\[\n0<B_k\\le\\frac1{\\sqrt2}<\\sqrt2\\qquad\\text{for all }k .\n\\]\n\nThus Alice never obtains a winning budget, but she always has a legal move (e.g. \\(a_k=0\\)).  By always playing \\(a_k=0\\) she forces \\(b_k=\\sqrt2\\) each round, and the equalities (8) and (9) become equalities; the game can continue indefinitely.  Hence neither player possesses a winning strategy.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Summary of strategies\n\n* **Alice (\\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)).**  \n  Play \\(a_k=0\\) as long as \\(B_k\\le\\sqrt2\\).  Because of (8) we have \\(S_{k-1}= (k-1)\\sqrt2\\); the inequality \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\) eventually holds, after which she chooses \\(a_k=B_k\\,(>\\sqrt2)\\) and wins immediately.\n\n* **Bazza (\\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)).**  \n  After each Alice move \\(a_k\\) respond with \\(b_k=\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}\\) (the maximal admissible value).  By (4) the total sum grows by at least \\(\\sqrt2\\) each round, so (9) forces \\(B_k\\) to become negative; Alice then has no legal move and loses.\n\n* **\\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).**  \n  The perpetual strategy \\(a_k=0,\\;b_k=\\sqrt2\\) satisfies all constraints forever; no player can force a win.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion\n\nThe critical value separating the two players\u2019 winning regions is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; \\text{Alice has a winning strategy},\\\\\n&\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; \\text{Bazza has a winning strategy},\\\\\n&\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; \\text{neither player can force a win (the game may last forever).}\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
