lice on round \\(k\\) by  \n\n\\[\nB_k:=\\lambda(2k-1)-S_{k-1}\\ge 0 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\) she can choose a number \\(a_k>B_k-\\sqrt2>\\sqrt2\\) and, by (5), the move is legal; then by Lemma 1 the subsequent Bazza‑move is impossible because it would require a negative square‑budget.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Alice wins on round }k\\iff B_k>\\sqrt2.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for the total sum\n\nFrom (4) we obtain, by induction,  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}\\ge k\\sqrt2\\qquad(k\\ge0).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIndeed \\(S_0=0\\); assuming \\(S_{k-1}\\ge (k-1)\\sqrt2\\) and using (4),\n\n\\[\nS_{k}=S_{k-1}+\\Delta_k\\ge (k-1)\\sqrt2+\\sqrt2=k\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Determination of the critical value of \\(\\lambda\\)\n\nCombine (6) with (8):\n\n\\[\nB_k\\le \\lambda(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*If* \\(\\lambda>\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then the right‑hand side of (9) grows linearly with slope \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2>0\\).  Consequently there exists a smallest integer \\(k\\) for which \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\); by (7) Alice wins on that round.  \n\n*If* \\(\\lambda<\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2<0\\) and the right‑hand side of (9) eventually becomes negative.  Hence for some \\(k\\) we have \\(B_k<0\\); Alice then has no legal move and loses, so Bazza wins.  \n\n*If* \\(\\lambda=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2=0\\) and (9) yields the constant bound  \n\n\\[\n0<B_k\\le\\frac1{\\sqrt2}<\\sqrt2\\qquad\\text{for all }k .\n\\]\n\nThus Alice never obtains a winning budget, but she always has a legal move (e.g. \\(a_k=0\\)).  By always playing \\(a_k=0\\) she forces \\(b_k=\\sqrt2\\) each round, and the equalities (8) and (9) become equalities; the game can continue indefinitely.  Hence neither player possesses a winning strategy.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Summary of strategies\n\n* **Alice (\\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)).**  \n  Play \\(a_k=0\\) as long as \\(B_k\\le\\sqrt2\\).  Because of (8) we have \\(S_{k-1}= (k-1)\\sqrt2\\); the inequality \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\) eventually holds, after which she chooses \\(a_k=B_k\\,(>\\sqrt2)\\) and wins immediately.\n\n* **Bazza (\\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)).**  \n  After each Alice move \\(a_k\\) respond with \\(b_k=\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}\\) (the maximal admissible value).  By (4) the total sum grows by at least \\(\\sqrt2\\) each round, so (9) forces \\(B_k\\) to become negative; Alice then has no legal move and loses.\n\n* **\\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).**  \n  The perpetual strategy \\(a_k=0,\\;b_k=\\sqrt2\\) satisfies all constraints forever; no player can force a win.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion\n\nThe critical value separating the two players’ winning regions is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; \\text{Alice has a winning strategy},\\\\\n&\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; \\text{Bazza has a winning strategy},\\\\\n&\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; \\text{neither player can force a win (the game may last forever).}\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
