"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have obtained a complete solution.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n*We analyse the game round\u2011by\u2011round (round\u202f\\(k\\) consists of Alice\u2019s odd move \\(a_k\\) and Bazza\u2019s even move \\(b_k\\)).*  \n\n* Lemma\u202f1: If the game reaches the end of round\u202f\\(k\\) then the sum of squares is exactly \\(Q_k=2k\\). Consequently Bazza can always answer Alice\u2019s move \\(a_k\\) (provided \\(a_k\\le\\sqrt2\\)) with  \n\\[\nb_k=\\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]  \n\n* The increase of the total sum in a round is  \n\\[\n\\Delta_k=a_k+b_k=a_k+\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}=:f(a_k),\\qquad0\\le a_k\\le\\sqrt2 .\n\\]  \n  The function \\(f\\) satisfies \\(\\sqrt2\\le f(a)\\le2\\) for all admissible \\(a\\); the minimum \\(\\sqrt2\\) is attained at \\(a=0\\) or \\(a=\\sqrt2\\). Hence each round adds at least \\(\\sqrt2\\) to the total sum.  \n\n* Let \\(S_{k}\\) be the total sum after round\u202f\\(k\\) and let  \n\\[\nB_k=\\lambda(2k-1)-S_{k-1}\n\\]  \nbe Alice\u2019s remaining budget before her move in round\u202f\\(k\\).  \nAlice can win on round\u202f\\(k\\) exactly when she can choose a number \\(a_k>\\sqrt2\\) while respecting (5); this is possible iff \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\).  \n\n* Using the minimal possible increase per round we obtain the sharp lower bound  \n\\[\nS_{k-1}\\ge (k-1)\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]  \nThus  \n\\[\nB_k\\le\\lambda(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]  \n\n* The inequality \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\) is equivalent to  \n\\[\n\\lambda(2k-1)>k\\sqrt2\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\lambda>\\frac{k\\sqrt2}{2k-1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]  \nThe right\u2011hand side decreases to \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}= \\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) as \\(k\\to\\infty\\).  \n\n* **If \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\):** choose \\(a_j=0\\) for all earlier rounds, so that equality holds in (2). For a sufficiently large integer \\(k\\) inequality (4) holds, whence \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\). Alice then selects \\(a_k=B_k\\;(>\\sqrt2)\\) and Bazza cannot respond; Alice wins.  \n\n* **If \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\):** Bazza follows the maximal response (1). Then each round adds at least \\(\\sqrt2\\) to the total sum, so (2) holds with equality. Because \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\), for large enough \\(k\\) we have \\(\\lambda(2k-1)<(k-1)\\sqrt2\\), i.e. \\(B_k<0\\). Alice then has no legal move and loses; Bazza wins.  \n\n* **If \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\):** the strategy \\(a_k=0,\\;b_k=\\sqrt2\\) for all \\(k\\) satisfies both players\u2019 constraints forever, so the game can be infinite. Moreover Alice can never make a move with \\(a_k>\\sqrt2\\) (her budget is always \\(1/\\sqrt2<\\sqrt2\\)), and Bazza cannot force a win because Alice\u2019s perpetual strategy prevents her from running out of budget. Hence neither player has a winning strategy.  \n\n**Final answer**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\lambda>\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\;:\\;\\text{Alice has a winning strategy},\\\\[6pt]\n\\lambda<\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\;:\\;\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy},\\\\[6pt]\n\\lambda=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\;:\\;\\text{neither player can force a win (the game may last forever).}\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]  \n\n\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet the game be played in **rounds** indexed by \\(k=1,2,\\dots\\).  \nIn round \\(k\\)\n\n* Alice (odd turn \\(n=2k-1\\)) chooses a non\u2011negative real number  \n  \\[\n  a_k:=x_{2k-1}.\n  \\]\n* Bazza (even turn \\(n=2k\\)) chooses a non\u2011negative real number  \n  \\[\n  b_k:=x_{2k}.\n  \\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(a_i+b_i)\\qquad\\text{(total sum after round }k\\text{)},\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_k:=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\bigl(a_i^{2}+b_i^{2}\\bigr)\\qquad\\text{(total sum of squares after round }k\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nThe constraints of the game become  \n\n\\[\n\\tag{A_k} S_{k-1}+a_k\\le\\lambda(2k-1),\\qquad\n\\tag{B_k} Q_{k-1}+a_k^{2}+b_k^{2}\\le 2k .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Exact square\u2011budget)  \nIf the game reaches the end of round \\(k\\) then  \n\\[\nQ_k=2k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* By induction.  For \\(k=0\\) the statement is true.  Assume it holds for \\(k-1\\); then \\(Q_{k-1}=2(k-1)\\).  After Alice\u2019s move the sum of squares is \\(2(k-1)+a_k^{2}\\).  Bazza can choose any \\(b_k\\ge0\\) satisfying  \n\\[\na_k^{2}+b_k^{2}\\le 2-0=2-a_k^{2}.\n\\]  \nTaking the maximal admissible value  \n\\[\nb_k=\\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\ngives \\(b_k^{2}=2-a_k^{2}\\) and therefore \\(Q_k=2(k-1)+a_k^{2}+b_k^{2}=2k\\).  \u220e  \n\nConsequences of Lemma\u202f1:\n\n* Alice\u2019s move must satisfy \\(a_k\\le\\sqrt2\\); otherwise \\(Q_{k-1}+a_k^{2}>2k\\) and Bazza would have no legal response.\n* Bazza can always answer Alice\u2019s admissible move by (1), which makes the square\u2011budget tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Increase of the total sum in a round  \n\nFrom (1) the increase of the total sum in round \\(k\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_k:=a_k+b_k\n      =a_k+\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}\n      =:f(a_k),\\qquad0\\le a_k\\le\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA short calculus argument shows  \n\n\\[\nf'(a)=1-\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}},\\qquad\nf'(a)=0\\iff a=1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(f\\) attains its **maximum** \\(f(1)=2\\) at \\(a=1\\) and its **minimum** at the endpoints:\n\\[\nf(0)=f(\\sqrt2)=\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\nThus for every admissible \\(a_k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt2\\le\\Delta_k\\le2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, after \\(k-1\\) completed rounds we have the lower bound  \n\n\\[\nS_{k-1}\\ge (k-1)\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (4) is achieved by the choice \\(a_j=0\\) (or \\(a_j=\\sqrt2\\)) for all \\(j<k\\), because then \\(b_j=\\sqrt2\\) and each round adds exactly \\(\\sqrt2\\) to the total sum.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Alice\u2019s remaining budget  \n\nBefore Alice\u2019s move in round \\(k\\) her **available budget** is  \n\n\\[\nB_k:=\\lambda(2k-1)-S_{k-1}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nShe must pick a non\u2011negative \\(a_k\\) with \\(a_k\\le B_k\\) (otherwise (A_k) would be violated).  \nIf she manages to choose a number **strictly larger than \\(\\sqrt2\\)**, then by Lemma\u202f1 Bazza cannot satisfy (B_k) and loses.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Alice can win on round }k\\iff B_k>\\sqrt2.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  When can Alice obtain a winning budget?  \n\nUsing the minimal possible total sum (4) in (5) gives the universal upper bound  \n\n\\[\nB_k\\le\\lambda(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus a necessary condition for Alice to win on round \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2>\\sqrt2\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n\\lambda(2k-1)>k\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquation (8) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda>\\frac{k\\sqrt2}{2k-1}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side of (9) is a decreasing function of \\(k\\) and satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\frac{k\\sqrt2}{2k-1}=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Winning strategy for Alice when \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nAssume \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).  \nChoose a large integer \\(k\\) satisfying (9); such a \\(k\\) exists because the right\u2011hand side of (9) tends to \\(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) from above.  \nAlice plays the **minimal\u2011sum strategy** in the first \\(k-1\\) rounds:\n\\[\na_j=0\\quad(j<k),\\qquad b_j=\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\nThen equality holds in (4), i.e. \\(S_{k-1}=(k-1)\\sqrt2\\).  \nBecause of (9) we have \\(B_k>\\sqrt2\\).  Alice now chooses  \n\n\\[\na_k:=B_k\\;(>\\sqrt2),\n\\]\n\nwhich respects (A_k).  Since \\(a_k>\\sqrt2\\), Lemma\u202f1 shows that Bazza cannot find any non\u2011negative \\(b_k\\) satisfying (B_k); consequently Bazza loses and **Alice wins**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Winning strategy for Bazza when \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nAssume \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).  \nBazza adopts the **maximal\u2011response strategy** (1) in every round:\n\\[\nb_k=\\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 this keeps the square\u2011budget tight (\\(Q_k=2k\\)) and guarantees the increase \\(\\Delta_k\\) satisfies the lower bound (3).  Hence (4) holds with equality:\n\\[\nS_{k-1}=(k-1)\\sqrt2\\quad\\text{for all }k.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\), the inequality opposite to (9) holds for all sufficiently large \\(k\\):\n\\[\n\\lambda(2k-1)<k\\sqrt2\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\lambda<\\frac{k\\sqrt2}{2k-1}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (4) we obtain for those \\(k\\)\n\n\\[\nB_k=\\lambda(2k-1)-S_{k-1}\n   =\\lambda(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2<0 .\n\\]\n\nThus Alice has **no legal move** on round \\(k\\) and loses.  Hence **Bazza wins**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The borderline case \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nTake the perpetual strategy  \n\n\\[\na_k=0,\\qquad b_k=\\sqrt2\\qquad(k=1,2,\\dots).\n\\]\n\nThen for every \\(k\\)\n\n\\[\nS_k=k\\sqrt2,\\qquad Q_k=2k,\n\\]\nand the constraints become  \n\n\\[\nS_{k-1}= (k-1)\\sqrt2 <\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(2k-1)=\\lambda(2k-1),\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_k=2k\\le 2k .\n\\]\n\nHence the game can continue indefinitely; neither player can force a win.\n\nMoreover, with \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) the remaining budget before Alice\u2019s move is always  \n\n\\[\nB_k=\\lambda(2k-1)-S_{k-1}\n    =\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(2k-1)-(k-1)\\sqrt2\n    =\\frac1{\\sqrt2}<\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\n\nThus Alice can never choose a number exceeding \\(\\sqrt2\\), so she cannot make Bazza lose.  Conversely, because Alice can always keep the minimal\u2011sum strategy (choose \\(a_k=0\\)), Bazza can never force her to run out of budget.  Consequently **no player has a winning strategy** when \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nThe analysis of the three regimes yields:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\lambda>\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\;:\\;\\text{Alice has a winning strategy},\\\\[6pt]\n\\lambda<\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\;:\\;\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy},\\\\[6pt]\n\\lambda=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\;:\\;\\text{neither player can force a win (the game may last forever).}\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
