                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe game is decided by the value of the parameter \\(\\lambda\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Alice has a winning strategy  iff }\\lambda\\ge \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy  iff }0<\\lambda<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(S_n=\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i\\) (total sum) and  \n   \\(Q_n=\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}\\) (total sum of squares).  \n\n2. **Alice’s possible win.**  \n   On an odd turn \\(n\\) she may set all the allowed sum into the current\n   move, i.e. choose \\(x_n=\\lambda n\\) and keep all previous moves equal to 0.\n   Then \\(S_n=\\lambda n\\) (so the constraint is tight) and  \n   \\(Q_n=(\\lambda n)^{2}\\).  \n   Bazza’s next move (turn \\(n+1\\)) must satisfy  \n   \\(Q_{n+1}=Q_n+x_{n+1}^{2}\\le n+1\\).  \n   Hence Alice wins as soon as \\((\\lambda n)^{2}>n+1\\).  \n   The smallest odd integer for which this holds is  \n\n   \\[\n   n_A:=\\min\\{\\,\\text{odd }n\\;:\\;\\lambda^{2}n^{2}>n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Bazza’s possible win.**  \n   After Alice’s move on turn \\(n-1\\) (odd) we have \\(S_{n-1}\\le\\lambda (n-1)\\).\n   On his even turn \\(n\\) Bazza wants to make Alice impossible to move on the\n   next odd turn.  This is achieved if after his move  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n>S_{n-1}+2\\lambda\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x_n>2\\lambda .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The only restriction on his choice is the square‑sum condition\n   \\(Q_n\\le n\\).  Since all previous squares can be kept at 0,\n   the maximal admissible value for \\(x_n\\) is \\(\\sqrt{n}\\).  \n   Consequently Bazza can realise (2) as soon as \\(\\sqrt{n}>2\\lambda\\),\n   i.e. for the smallest even integer\n\n   \\[\n   n_B:=\\min\\{\\,\\text{even }n\\;:\\;n>4\\lambda^{2}\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Comparison of the two critical indices.**  \n   The game ends at the earlier of the two turns \\(n_A\\) and \\(n_B\\).\n   Using (1) and (3) one checks that  \n\n   \\[\n   n_A<n_B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\lambda^{2}>\\frac12\n   \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n   n_B<n_A\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\lambda^{2}<\\frac12 .\n   \\]\n\n   The borderline case \\(\\lambda^{2}=1/2\\) gives \\(n_A=3\\) and \\(n_B=4\\);\n   thus Alice still wins.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   * If \\(\\lambda\\ge 1/\\sqrt2\\) then \\(n_A<n_B\\); Alice’s strategy described\n     in step 2 forces a win on turn \\(n_A\\).  \n   * If \\(0<\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\) then \\(n_B<n_A\\); Bazza’s strategy of step 3\n     forces a win on turn \\(n_B\\).  \n\nThe two strategies are optimal: each player can always keep the opponent’s\ncritical quantity (the sum of squares for Bazza, the total sum for Alice)\nas low as possible until the decisive turn, so the above thresholds are\nsharp.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i ,\\qquad   \nQ_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}\\qquad (n\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nThe rules are  \n\n* for odd \\(n\\): Alice must choose \\(x_n\\ge0\\) with \\(S_n\\le\\lambda n\\);\n* for even \\(n\\): Bazza must choose \\(x_n\\ge0\\) with \\(Q_n\\le n\\).\n\nIf a player cannot make a legal choice, the other player wins.\nAll numbers are non‑negative, hence the sequences \\((S_n)\\) and \\((Q_n)\\) are\nnon‑decreasing.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Alice’s winning strategy\n\nAssume the current turn number \\(n\\) is odd.\nAlice may set all previously chosen numbers to \\(0\\) and pick\n\n\\[\nx_n:=\\lambda n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\lambda n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad Q_n=(\\lambda n)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe choice (4) respects Alice’s constraint because \\(S_n=\\lambda n\\) is\nexactly the allowed bound.\n\nOn the following even turn \\(n+1\\) Bazza must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nQ_{n+1}=Q_n+x_{n+1}^{2}\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((\\lambda n)^{2}>n+1\\) then (5) is already impossible, because the left‑hand\nside is at least \\(Q_n=(\\lambda n)^{2}>n+1\\) even before adding the non‑negative\nterm \\(x_{n+1}^{2}\\).  Hence Bazza cannot move and loses.\n\nConsequently Alice wins as soon as an odd integer \\(n\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda^{2}n^{2}>n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn_A:=\\min\\{\\,\\text{odd }n\\;:\\;\\lambda^{2}n^{2}>n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause the left‑hand side of (6) grows like \\(n^{2}\\) while the right‑hand side\ngrows only linearly, such an odd \\(n\\) always exists for every \\(\\lambda>0\\).\nAlice’s strategy is to keep all earlier moves equal to \\(0\\) and on turn\n\\(n_A\\) to play (4).  She then wins immediately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Bazza’s winning strategy\n\nSuppose the current turn number \\(n\\) is even.\nLet \\(S_{n-1}\\) be the sum after Alice’s previous (odd) move.\nBecause Alice’s last move satisfied \\(S_{n-1}\\le\\lambda (n-1)\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nS_{n-1}\\le\\lambda (n-1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBazza wants to make Alice’s next move impossible.\nOn the next odd turn \\(n+1\\) Alice must have  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}=S_n+x_{n+1}\\le\\lambda (n+1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_{n+1}\\ge0\\), a necessary condition for (9) is  \n\n\\[\nS_n\\le\\lambda (n+1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus Bazza wins if he can ensure  \n\n\\[\nS_n>S_{n-1}+2\\lambda .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8) this is guaranteed whenever he chooses  \n\n\\[\nx_n>2\\lambda .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe only restriction on his choice is the square‑sum condition\n\\(Q_n\\le n\\).  By keeping all previous squares equal to \\(0\\) (Alice can\nalways do this by playing \\(0\\) on her odd turns), the maximal admissible\nvalue for \\(x_n\\) is \\(\\sqrt{n}\\).  Hence (12) is realizable as soon as  \n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{n}>2\\lambda\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad n>4\\lambda^{2}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn_B:=\\min\\{\\,\\text{even }n\\;:\\;n>4\\lambda^{2}\\,\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBazza’s strategy is therefore:\n\n* keep all his previous moves equal to \\(0\\) (so that \\(Q_{n-1}=0\\));\n* on turn \\(n_B\\) choose any \\(x_{n_B}>2\\lambda\\) (for instance\n  \\(x_{n_B}=\\sqrt{n_B}\\), which is allowed by (13)).\n\nThen (12) holds, (10) fails, and Alice cannot make a legal move on turn\n\\(n_B+1\\).  Hence Bazza wins at turn \\(n_B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of the winner\n\nThe game ends at the earlier of the two critical turns \\(n_A\\) (Alice’s win)\nand \\(n_B\\) (Bazza’s win).  We compare them.\n\nFrom (7) the inequality \\(\\lambda^{2}n^{2}>n+1\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nn>\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1+4\\lambda^{2}}}{2\\lambda^{2}}\n\\approx\\frac{1}{\\lambda^{2}}\\quad(\\text{large }n).\n\\]\n\nThus, asymptotically,\n\\[\nn_A\\approx\\frac{1}{\\lambda^{2}}\\quad\\text{(odd)} .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFrom (14) we have  \n\n\\[\nn_B\\approx 4\\lambda^{2}\\quad\\text{(even)} .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(n_A<n_B\\) precisely when  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\lambda^{2}}<4\\lambda^{2}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n\\lambda^{4}>\\frac14\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n\\lambda>\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(n_A<n_B\\); Alice’s strategy described in\nSection 1 wins on turn \\(n_A\\).\n\nIf \\(0<\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(n_B<n_A\\); Bazza’s strategy of\nSection 2 wins on turn \\(n_B\\).\n\nAt the borderline \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) we have\n\\(\\lambda^{2}=1/2\\).  Equation (6) gives \\(n_A=3\\) (the smallest odd integer\nwith \\((\\lambda n)^{2}>n+1\\)), while (13) yields \\(n_B=4\\) (the smallest even\ninteger with \\(n>4\\lambda^{2}=2\\)).  Thus \\(n_A<n_B\\) and Alice still wins.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Alice has a winning strategy  iff }\\lambda\\ge\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}},\n\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy  iff }0<\\lambda<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth strategies are optimal: each player can keep the opponent’s critical\nquantity (the total sum for Alice, the total sum of squares for Bazza) as low\nas possible until the decisive turn, and the thresholds above are sharp."
