"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Alice has a winning strategy  iff }\\lambda>\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy  iff }\\lambda<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}} .\n\\]\n\nFor the critical value \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) neither player can force a win; with optimal play the game can be continued forever.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(S_n=\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i\\) (total sum) and  \n   \\(Q_n=\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}\\) (total sum of squares).  \n\n2. **A bound for the even\u2011player\u2019s total sum.**  \n   Let \\(E_k=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}\\) be the sum of Bazza\u2019s first \\(k\\) moves.  \n   By Cauchy\u2013Schwarz,\n   \\[\n   E_k^{2}\\le k\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}^{2}\\le k\\cdot(2k)=2k^{2},\n   \\]\n   because after the \\(2k\\)-th turn the square\u2011sum constraint gives\n   \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}^{2}\\le2k\\).  \n   Hence\n   \\[\n   E_k\\le\\sqrt{2}\\,k. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Alice\u2019s possible win.**  \n   After the \\((2k+1)\\)-st (odd) turn the total square sum is\n   \\[\n   Q_{2k+1}=Q_{2k}+x_{2k+1}^{2},\n   \\]\n   where \\(Q_{2k}\\le2k\\) by the even\u2011turn constraint.\n   If Alice can make\n   \\[\n   x_{2k+1}^{2}>2, \\qquad\\text{i.e. }x_{2k+1}>\\sqrt2,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   then \\(Q_{2k+1}>2k+2\\) and Bazza cannot satisfy his next\n   square\u2011sum condition; Alice wins immediately.\n\n   The largest value Alice can give to \\(x_{2k+1}\\) is limited by the\n   odd\u2011turn sum constraint:\n   \\[\n   S_{2k+1}=O_{k+1}+E_k\\le\\lambda(2k+1),\n   \\]\n   where \\(O_{k+1}=\\sum_{j=0}^{k}x_{2j+1}\\) is the total of Alice\u2019s odd moves.\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   x_{2k+1}\\le O_{k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)-E_k.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the worst\u2011case bound (1) for Bazza,\n   \\[\n   O_{k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)-\\sqrt2\\,k.\n   \\]\n   Thus a sufficient condition for (2) to become possible is\n   \\[\n   \\lambda(2k+1)-\\sqrt2\\,k>\\sqrt2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Inequality (4) is equivalent to\n   \\[\n   k\\bigl(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\bigr)>\\sqrt2-\\lambda .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   If \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2>0\\) and\n   (5) holds for all sufficiently large \\(k\\).  Hence Alice can\n   eventually choose an odd move satisfying (2) and win.\n\n4. **Bazza\u2019s possible win.**  \n   Bazza wants to make Alice unable to move on some odd turn.\n   From (3) and (1) we have the maximal odd\u2011turn allowance\n   \\[\n   O_{k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)-\\sqrt2\\,k .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   If \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then the right\u2013hand side of (6) becomes\n   negative for all sufficiently large \\(k\\); consequently Alice would be\n   forced to choose a negative \\(x_{2k+1}\\), which is illegal.  Therefore\n   before that point Bazza can already make the odd\u2011turn inequality\n   impossible, and Alice loses.\n\n   To realise the worst case (1) Bazza simply sets each even move to the\n   constant value\n   \\[\n   x_{2j}=\\sqrt2\\qquad(j\\ge1),\n   \\]\n   which satisfies his square\u2011sum constraint with equality:\n   \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}^{2}=2k\\).  Hence the bound (1) is attainable,\n   and the argument above is sharp.\n\n5. **The critical value \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).**  \n   When \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) we have \\(2\\lambda=\\sqrt2\\), so the\n   left\u2011hand side of (5) is identically zero while the right\u2011hand side is\n   positive; inequality (5) never holds.  Thus Alice can never achieve\n   (2).  On the other hand, with the constant even moves \\(x_{2j}=\\sqrt2\\)\n   the odd\u2011turn allowance (6) equals exactly \\(\\lambda\\), so Alice can\n   always choose \\(x_{2k+1}=\\lambda\\) and satisfy the sum constraint.\n   Consequently neither player can force a win; with optimal play the\n   game can be continued forever.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   * If \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) Alice can force a win (strategy: keep\n     Bazza\u2019s even moves as small as possible, then on a sufficiently\n     large odd turn make a single odd move larger than \\(\\sqrt2\\)).  \n   * If \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) Bazza can force a win (strategy:\n     always play \\(x_{2j}=\\sqrt2\\); this eventually makes the odd\u2011turn\n     sum bound negative, so Alice cannot move).  \n   * For \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) neither player has a winning\n     strategy; the game can be made infinite.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i ,\\qquad   \nQ_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}\\qquad (n\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nThe rules are  \n\n* for odd \\(n\\): Alice must choose \\(x_n\\ge0\\) with \\(S_n\\le\\lambda n\\);\n* for even \\(n\\): Bazza must choose \\(x_n\\ge0\\) with \\(Q_n\\le n\\).\n\nIf a player cannot make a legal choice, the other player wins; if the\ngame never ends, there is no winner.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound on Bazza\u2019s total sum\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nE_k:=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}\\qquad(k\\ge1)\n\\]\n\nthe sum of Bazza\u2019s first \\(k\\) moves.  \nAfter the \\(2k\\)-th turn the square\u2011sum condition gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}^{2}\\le 2k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBy Cauchy\u2013Schwarz,\n\n\\[\nE_k^{2}\\le k\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}^{2}\\le k\\cdot(2k)=2k^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{E_k\\le\\sqrt2\\,k}\\qquad\\text{for all }k\\ge1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe bound (2) is sharp: taking \\(x_{2j}=\\sqrt2\\) for every \\(j\\) makes\n\\(E_k=\\sqrt2\\,k\\) and satisfies (1) with equality.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  When can Alice force a win?\n\nConsider an odd turn \\(n=2k+1\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nO_{k+1}:=\\sum_{j=0}^{k}x_{2j+1}\n\\]\n\nbe the total of Alice\u2019s odd moves up to this turn.  The odd\u2011turn rule\nrequires  \n\n\\[\nO_{k+1}+E_k=S_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1),\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\nO_{k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)-E_k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe total square sum after this turn is  \n\n\\[\nQ_{2k+1}=Q_{2k}+x_{2k+1}^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhere by the even\u2011turn constraint \\(Q_{2k}\\le2k\\).  If Alice can make  \n\n\\[\nx_{2k+1}>\\sqrt2,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nthen  \n\n\\[\nQ_{2k+1}>2k+2,\n\\]\n\nand Bazza cannot satisfy his next square\u2011sum condition; Alice wins\nimmediately.\n\nBecause the sum of squares of Alice\u2019s odd moves is at most\n\\(O_{k+1}^{2}\\) (the maximum is attained when all of \\(O_{k+1}\\) is placed\nin a single odd term), condition (5) is achievable precisely when\n\\(O_{k+1}>\\sqrt2\\).  Using the worst\u2011case bound (2) for Bazza in (4) we\nobtain the sufficient inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda(2k+1)-\\sqrt2\\,k>\\sqrt2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInequality (6) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\bigr)>\\sqrt2-\\lambda .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2>0\\), and\n(7) holds for all sufficiently large \\(k\\).  Hence Alice can, after a\nfinite number of rounds, choose an odd move satisfying (5) and win.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  When can Bazza force a win?\n\nBazza\u2019s aim is to make Alice unable to move on some odd turn.  From (3) we\nhave the necessary condition for Alice\u2019s move at turn \\(2k+1\\):\n\n\\[\nO_{k+1}\\ge0\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nO_{k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)-E_k .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIf the right\u2013hand side of (8) becomes negative, Alice cannot choose a\nnon\u2011negative \\(x_{2k+1}\\) and loses.  Using the attainable bound (2) for\n\\(E_k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\lambda(2k+1)-\\sqrt2\\,k<0\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\bigl(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\bigr)<-\\lambda .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) we have \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2<0\\),\nso the left side of (9) eventually becomes smaller than \\(-\\lambda\\);\nhence for all sufficiently large \\(k\\) the quantity\n\\(\\lambda(2k+1)-E_k\\) is negative.  Consequently Alice will be unable to\nmake a legal odd move and Bazza wins.\n\nBazza can indeed realise the bound \\(E_k=\\sqrt2\\,k\\) by playing\n\n\\[\nx_{2j}=\\sqrt2\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots).\n\\]\n\nThese moves satisfy the square\u2011sum constraint with equality:\n\\(\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}^{2}=2k\\).  Therefore the argument above is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The critical value \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nIf \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) then \\(2\\lambda=\\sqrt2\\).  Inequality (7)\nreduces to \\(0>\\sqrt2-\\lambda>0\\), which is never true; thus Alice can\nnever achieve \\(x_{2k+1}>\\sqrt2\\) and cannot force a win.\n\nWith Bazza playing \\(x_{2j}=\\sqrt2\\) we have \\(E_k=\\sqrt2\\,k\\).  From (8)\n\n\\[\nO_{k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)-\\sqrt2\\,k\n     =\\frac{2k+1}{\\sqrt2}-\\sqrt2\\,k\n     =\\frac1{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nHence Alice can always choose the maximal odd move\n\\(x_{2k+1}=\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\), keeping the sum exactly at the\nallowed bound and never violating her own rule.  Consequently Bazza\ncannot make Alice lose, and Alice cannot make Bazza lose.  With both\nplayers following these optimal strategies the game proceeds forever,\nso neither player has a winning strategy.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final classification\n\n* **If \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\):**  \n  Alice can force a win by keeping Bazza\u2019s even moves as small as\n  possible and, after a sufficiently large odd turn, making a single odd\n  move larger than \\(\\sqrt2\\).\n\n* **If \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\):**  \n  Bazza can force a win by always playing \\(x_{2j}=\\sqrt2\\); this makes\n  the odd\u2011turn allowance eventually negative, so Alice cannot move.\n\n* **If \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\):**  \n  Neither player can force a win; the game can be continued indefinitely\n  (draw).\n\nThus the set of \\(\\lambda\\) for which Alice has a winning strategy is\n\\((\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\infty)\\); the set for which Bazza has a winning\nstrategy is \\((0,\\frac1{\\sqrt2})\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Alice wins iff }\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\text{Bazza wins iff }\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}} .\n\\]"
